messages:
sam.k: Heads-up — I opened a PR to switch the inference gateway TLS wiring from per-Service cert annotations to Gateway API + SecretBinding + TLSRoute SNI rewrite. Planning to run terraform apply in ~1h unless there's a hard blocker.

liz.p: Can you paste the relevant TF snippet and the plan output? my main concerns: healthcheck targetType, NLB passthrough semantics, and whether we preserve nodeport binding. :thinking:

sam.k: Sure — excerpt from the PR:```hcl
resource "kubernetes_manifest" "gateway_inference_tls" {
  manifest = {
    apiVersion = "gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1beta1"
    kind       = "SecretBinding"
    metadata = { name = "inference-tls-binding" }
    spec = {
      secretRef = { name = "inference-cert" }
      protocol = "TLS"
    }
  }
}

resource "kubernetes_manifest" "inference_tlsroute" {
  manifest = {
    apiVersion = "gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1beta1"
    kind = "TLSRoute"
    metadata = { name = "inference-tlsroute" }
    spec = {
      hostnames = ["inference.redwood.ai"]
      rules = [{ matches = [{ sni = "inference.redwood.ai" }], forwardTo = [{ serviceName = "inference-svc", port = 8080 }] }]
    }
  }
}
```

Plan preview (relevant bits):
```
+ kubernetes_manifest.gateway_inference_tls (new resource)
+ kubernetes_manifest.inference_tlsroute (new resource)
~ kubernetes_service.inference-svc (update)
    metadata.annotations:    "service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-ssl-cert" => removed
    spec.externalTrafficPolicy: "Cluster" => "Local" (no change to port)
```

sam.k: TL;DR: we remove the ALB cert annotation and rely on the Gateway API binding. NLB passthrough paths should remain since the service still exposes the same port; we only change how certs are associated.

rohan: Quick checks: 1) SecretBinding needs the namespace of the TLS secret — make sure `secretRef` points to the right ns, otherwise the bind will be a no-op. 2) Who owns the secret rotation IAM role now? If we switch to Gateway API, the controller/serviceaccount that does the secret-to-lb mapping must have read access. 3) How will this affect existing Canaries?

sam.k: Good catches. I added `secretRef.namespace = "redwood-inference"` in the PR. For rotation, the controller SA is `gateway-controller@infra` which currently has KMS decrypt; I'll add a small IAM policy change in the same PR. Canaries: ops-bot will create a canary cluster route first (see below).

liz.p: Healthchecks: confirm probes still hit the same target port (8080) and we don't switch to `targetType=ip` accidentally. If ExternalTrafficPolicy flips to Local we need to ensure pod readiness is solid or healthchecks will fail on empty nodes.

sam.k: Affirmed — spec.targetPort remains 8080, and I'm intentionally keeping `externalTrafficPolicy=Cluster` to avoid node-local failures for the baseline apply. I'll follow up with a small canary that toggles Local only after we validate health.

ops-bot: [automated] Canary scheduled: cluster=inference-canary, start=2025-03-15T12:30:00Z, steps=[apply-gateway-secretbinding, apply-tlsroute, smoke-http-checks]. Failure threshold=2/3.

rohan: Also add a short tfstate migration note in the PR describing deleted annotation -> new SecretBinding mapping, to help anyone debugging drift. And a post-apply step: run `kubectl get gateway -A -o yaml | grep -E "inference-tls-binding|inference-tlsroute"` to validate objects.

liz.p: LGTM from infra perspective. One tiny nit: include a note in the PR about rollback steps (re-apply service annotation + remove TLSRoute) so support can quickly revert if the controller misbehaves.

sam.k: Will do both (tfstate note + rollback steps). Thanks — I'll wait 1h for any more objections then run apply against the canary and report back here.

ops-bot: [automated] Reminder: terraform plan lock will expire in 45m unless refreshed.

sam.k: Got it. ping me if you see anything after I kick off the canary. Thanks all!
